<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section-6-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Comparing two proportions: Theory-based approach</title>

<subsection> 
 <title>Donating blood</title>
<p>Blood donations from volunteers serve a crucial role in the nation's health care system. 
Are Americans any more or less generous about donating blood in some years than others? Are
 women any more or less willing to give blood than men? To investigate these questions we can 
 analyze data from the <url href="https://gss.norc.org/">General Social Survey</url>, which 
 is a national survey conducted every two years on a nationwide random sample of adult Americans.
</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 1-->
<statement>
<p>Did this study make use of random sampling, random assignment, both, or neither? Also explain 
what your answer means in terms of the scope of conclusions that can be drawn from these data.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Random sampling only, this means we can generalize to the population of all Adult Americans.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>Let's start by comparing the years 2002 and 2004.</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 2-->
<statement>
<p>State the appropriate null and alternative hypotheses, in words and in symbols, to address 
the research question of whether Americans were more generous with blood donations in one of 
these years than the other. (Hint: Remember that hypotheses are always about population parameters, 
and think about whether the alternative should be one- or two-sided before you see the data.)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Null: The proportion of all US adults who gave blood in 2002 is the same as in 2004
  </p>
  <p>
    Alternative: The proportion of all US adults who gave blood in 2002 is different than in 2004
  </p>
  <p>
    Null: <m>\pi_{2002} - \pi_{2004} = 0</m>
  </p>
  <p>
    Alternative:  <m>\pi_{2002} - \pi_{2004} \not = 0</m>
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>Data from the 2002 and 2004 surveys are summarized in this two-way table:
</p>
<table>
  <tabular>
    <row>
      <cell>
      </cell>
      <cell>
        2002
      </cell>
      <cell>
        2004
      </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>
        Donated blood in past year
      </cell>
      <cell>
        210
      </cell>
      <cell>
        230
      </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>
        Have not donated blood in past year
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,152
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,106
      </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>
        Total
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,362
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,336
      </cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
</table>

<activity>
 <!--Question 3-->
<statement>
<p>For each year, calculate the (conditional) proportion of adult Americans sampled who donated 
blood in the previous 12 months. Use appropriate symbols to represent them. Also calculate the 
difference between these sample proportions (taking the 2002 proportion minus the 2004 proportion).</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    <m>\hat{p}_{2002} = 0.154</m> and <m>\hat{p}_{2004} = 0.172</m> which gives us  <m>\hat{p}_{2002} - \hat{p}_{2004} = -0.018</m>
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 4-->
<statement>
  <p>Produce a segmented bar graph or mosaic plot to display the conditional proportions who gave
  blood in these two years. Comment on what the graph and your calculations from question 3 reveal about these two samples.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The proportion of donors in 2002 is very close to the proportion of donors in 2004.
  </p>
  <image source="6-3/bars.png" width="70%">
    <description>bar chart showing that <m>\hat{p}_{2002} = 0.154</m> and <m>\hat{p}_{2004} = 0.172</m></description>
  </image>
  <image source="6-3/mosaic.png" width="50%">
    <description>mosaic chart showing <m>\hat{p}_{2002} = 0.154</m> and <m>\hat{p}_{2004} = 0.172</m></description>
  </image>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <statement>
  <!--Question 5-->
<p>Use the <url href="http://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/chisqshuffle/ChiSqShuffle.htm?hideExtras=2">
  Multiple Proportions applet</url> to conduct a simulation analysis to approximate a p-value for 
  testing the hypotheses that you stated in question 2. You can enter the table of counts in the 
  <m>2\times 2</m> table or type in the table in the <term>Sample data</term> box and press <term>Use Table</term>. 
  Or you can copy the data from the text webpage and paste into the <term>Sample data</term> box and press 
  <term>Use Data</term>. Verify the segmented bar graph that you created.
  </p>
<image source="6-3/screenshot.png" width="50%">
<description>A screenshot of the multiple proportions tool
</description>
</image>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Instructions for entering a table in the Two Proportions applet
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<remark> 
<p>Shuffling is an appropriate way to estimate a p-value for comparing groups regardless of 
the study design (observational study or experiment, random assignment or random sampling or 
neither). Of course, the study design is very important in determining the appropriate scope 
of conclusions. (Can you generalize to a larger population? Can you infer a 
cause-and-effect conclusion?)
</p>
</remark>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 6-->
 <introduction>
  <p>
    Check the <term>Show Shuffle Options</term> box, ask for 1000 repetitions, and press 
    <term>Shuffle</term>.
  </p>
 </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Indicate how to find the p-value from the null distribution. (Hint: Remember whether the alternative hypothesis is one-sided or two-sided.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The proportion of simulated differences in proportions smaller than -0.018, or larger than 0.018.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Report the approximate p-value.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Approximate p-value = 0.19
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Based on this p-value, do the sample data provide much evidence that the population 
        proportion who gave blood in 2002 differs from the population proportion who gave 
        blood in 2004?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We do not have evidence that the proportion of blood donors in 2002 is different than in 2004.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 7-->
<statement>
<p>Does the null distribution of the simulated values appear to follow an approximately normal distribution? Centered around what value? Also report the standard deviation of these values.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Yes, approximately normal. Mean = 0, standard deviation = 0.014
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 8-->
  <introduction>
    <p>Let's see what the standardized statistic tells us.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Report the observed value in the sample for the statistic.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        -0.018.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        How many standard deviations (based on your answer to question 7) above 
        or below zero (the hypothesized value) is the observed value of the statistic?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        -0.018/0.014 = -1.29.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Is the strength of evidence against the null hypothesis based on this standardized 
        statistic consistent with what the p-value revealed in question 6? Explain.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes, the strength of evidence based on the standardized statistic is consistent. The standardized statistic is not very large (it is between -1.5 and 1.5), so we do not have evidence of a significant difference.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<p>Just as you learned a theory-based approach for making inferences about a single proportion, 
so too will you now learn a theory-based approach for making inferences about comparing 
proportions between two groups. This theory-based approach is again based on an approximation 
from a normal distribution.
</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 9-->
<statement>
<p>Check the Overlay Normal distribution box. Is the behavior of the two distributions similar? How do the p-values compare?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The normal distribution overlays fairly well with the simulated null distribution. p-value = 0.2058, similar to what we got before.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 10-->
  <introduction>
    <p>Check the box to compute <term>95% CI(s) for difference in proportions</term>. (This box appears 
    in the left-hand panel when the difference is selected as the statistic.)</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Report the endpoints of this interval.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        (-0.046, 0.01)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Interpret this interval. Be sure to mention clearly what parameter is being estimated 
        by this interval. (Hint: In other words, you are 95% confident that this interval contains what?)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We are 95% confident that the parameter (difference in true proportion of blood donors in 2002 and 2004) is between -0.046 and 0.01.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Does this interval contain only negative values, only positive values, or does the interval 
        include the value zero as well as both positive and negative values? Comment on the 
        importance of what this means.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The interval contains both positive and negative values. This means that 0 is a plausible value for the difference in the two proportions. In other words, we don't have evidence to reject the null hypothesis that the two proportions are the same.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 11-->
 <statement>
  <p>
    Another applet that can compute p-values and confidence intervals for tests of two
    proportions is the <url href="http://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/tbia/TBIA.html?hideExtras=1">Theory-Based Inference applet</url>. Use the pull-down menu to select
    <term>Two proportions</term> and enter the relevant summary information, using the 2002 sample
    as Group 1. Check the <term>Test of Significance</term> box and press on the <term>inequality</term> sign
    button until the alternative hypothesis becomes two-sided. Press <term>Calculate</term>. Verify the
    p-value you obtained earlier (question 9) as well as the confidence interval (question 10).
  </p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
  <p>
    Calculations verified. Same p-value and confidence interval.
  </p>
 </solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Validity Conditions for Theory-Based Approach (Two Proportions)</title>
<p>As with a single proportion, the theory-based inference approach for comparing two proportions 
is only valid when a normal distribution provides a good approximation to the distribution of the 
statistic <m> \hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2 </m>. Conservatively, the theory-based significance test and 
confidence interval work well when there are at least 10 observations in each of the four cells 
of the two-way table. In other words, we require at least 10 "successes" and at least 10 "failures" 
in each sample in order to use this theory-based approach.
</p>
<convention>
<p>The theory-based test and interval for the difference in two proportions (called a 
<term>two-sample z-test or interval</term>) work well when there are at least 10 
observations in each of the four cells of the <m>2\times 2</m> table.
</p>
</convention>

<activity>
 <!--Question 12-->
<statement>
<p>Is it valid to use the theory-based approach for two proportions in this study? Justify
your answer.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Yes, 210, 1152, 230 and 1106 are all larger than 10—thus the validity conditions are met.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 13-->
<statement>
<p>Is it reasonable to generalize your conclusions from this analysis to the population of all adult Americans? Explain why or why not.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Yes, because the data is from a random sample of Americans.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Exploring further</title>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 14-->
  <introduction>
    <p>Let's see what will happen if we switch which is Group 1 and which is Group 2.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Before doing any calculations, what do you think would happen to the standardized statistic, 
        p-value, and confidence interval if you entered the 2004 sample as Group 1 and the 2002 
        sample as Group 2?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Standardized statistic would switch signs, p-value would remain the same, confidence interval would be same width, but centered at 0.018 instead of -0.018
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Make this change in the Theory-Based Inference applet (i.e., switch the roles of Groups 
        1 and 2), and then press Calculate. Report the new values of the standardized statistic, 
        p-value, and confidence interval.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        1.27, p-value = 0.21, confidence interval (-0.01, 0.046)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Did these change as you predicted in part (a)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<p>Using the General Social Survey data to classify respondents by sex and combining the years 2002 and 2004 we can construct a two-way table:
</p>
<table>
  <tabular>
    <row>
      <cell>
      </cell>
      <cell>
        Male
      </cell>
      <cell>
        Female
      </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>
        Donated blood in past year
      </cell>
      <cell>
        239
      </cell>
      <cell>
        201
      </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>
        Have not donated blood in past year
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,032
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,226
      </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>
        Total
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,271
      </cell>
      <cell>
        1,427
      </cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
</table>

<activity>
 <!--Question 15-->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Analyze these data to address the question of whether American men and women differ with regard to donating blood.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Are the validity conditions met?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The validity conditions are met (239, 1032, 201 and 1226 are all larger than 10)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the null and alternative hypothesis using full sentences.  (Do males - females)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Null hypothesis: The proportion of U.S. male blood donors is the same as the proportion of U.S. female blood donors.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alternative hypothesis: The proportion of U.S. male blood donors is different than the proportion of U.S. female blood donors.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the proportion of male blood donors and female blood donors.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The proportion of male blood donors is 239/1271=0.188, females is 201/1427=0.141
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Create a chart that compares these two proportions.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <image source="6-3/bars-sex.png" width="70%">
        <description>Bar chart showing the proportion of male blood donors is 239/1271=0.188, females is 201/1427=0.141</description>
      </image>
      <image source="6-3/mosaic-sex.png" width="50%">
        <description>Mosaic chart showing the proportion of male blood donors is 239/1271=0.188, females is 201/1427=0.141</description>
      </image>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the standardized statistic and the p-value.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Standardized statistic = 3.31, p-value = 0.0009.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Make a conclusion regarding your null and alternative hypothesis in light of the p-value you calculated.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We have very strong evidence that the proportion of male blood donors is different than the proportion of female blood donors.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate a 95% confidence interval for the difference between the proportion of each gender who donate blood.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        (0.019, 0.075)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Interpret your confidence interval, giving all necessary context.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We are 95% confident that the proportion of males in the U.S. who donate blood is between 0.019 and 0.075 higher than the proportion of females in the U.S. who donate blood.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

</subsection>

</section>